Aguiar and Ana Paula S. Dias. The lattice of synchrony subspaces of a coupled cell network: characterization and computation algorithm. J. Nonlinear Sci., 24(6):949–996, 2014.\n\n[2] Hiroko Kamei and Peter J. A. Cock. Computation of balanced equivalence relations and their lattice for a coupled cell network. SIAM J. Appl. Dyn. Syst., 12(1):352\u0015382, 2013.\n\n[3] Ian Stewart, Martin Golubitsky, and Marcus Pivato. Symmetry groupoids and patterns of synchrony in coupled cell networks. SIAM J. Appl. Dyn. Syst. 2 (2003), no. 4, 609–646\n\n2019-08-27 15:40:59 MST--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n22 — JOHN M. NEUBERGER, NÁNDOR SIEBEN, AND JAMES W. SWIFT (https://jan.ucc.nau.edu/jmn3/papers/nonlin.pdf)\nciteturn0search12 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; NEUBERGER, NÁNDOR SIEBEN, AND JAMES W. SWIFT ... These two solutions are included for completeness, but their existence for the PDE would have to be confirmed with more modes and a higher level approximation of the eigenfunctions.\n\nSpecifically, the existence theorem in [4] assumes that there is a unitary representation \\(\\{T(k)\\}_{k\\in G}\\) of a compact topological group \\(G\\), that the corresponding fixed point subspace \\(\\operatorname{Fix}(G)\\) is nontrivial and closed by the taking of positive and negative parts, and that \\(J\\) is invariant under the representation: \\(J(T(k)u)=J(u)\\) for all \\(u\\in H\\) and \\(k\\in G\\).\n</visual_element>\n\n**Figure 10.** A partial bifurcation diagram containing solutions of the seven remaining symmetry types. Primary branch 24 is the first branch with symmetry type \\(S_2\\). The symmetry types \\(S_{14}\\) and \\(S_{11}\\) were found by searching the first one hundred primary branches, following only those branches which can lead to solutions with the desired symmetry. These two solutions are included for completeness, but their existence for the PDE would have to be confirmed with more modes and a higher level approximation of the eigenfunctions.\n\nSpecifically, the existence theorem in [4] assumes that there is a unitary representation \\(\\{T(k)\\}_{k\\in G}\\) of a compact topological group \\(G\\), that the corresponding fixed point subspace \\(\\operatorname{Fix}(G)\\) is nontrivial and closed by the taking of positive and negative parts, and that \\(J\\) is invariant under the representation: \\(J(T(k)u)=J(u)\\) for all \\(u\\in H\\) and \\(k\\in G\\). As a result, it is shown that there exists a sign-changing solution in \\(\\chi=\\operatorname{Fix}(G)\\).\n\nSeveral facts are clear. One, the finite dimensional group-theoretic vector results in the present paper as applied to the grid certainly translate to the infinite dimensional function setting. That is to say, each of our 21 nontrivial symmetry types provides such a setting. Secondly, the averaging used in the MMPA in [4] to gain membership to the invariant subspace \\(\\operatorname{Fix}(G)\\) is a specific application of our theoretical projection techniques; our eigenfunction decomposition and corresponding symmetry analysis makes this process now particularly easy. Also, the proof can be easily adapted to the (unmodified) mountain pass setting; while originally used to obtain one-sign solutions, if a given isotropy group inherently implies that the invariant space contains sign-changing elements then minimization of \\(J|_{\\chi\\cap W}\\) will yield sign-changing solutions.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCrossmark (https://crossmark.crossref.org/dialog/?doi=10.1007%2Fs10601-024-09378-3)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Invariant polydiagonal subspaces of matrices and constraint programming ... Neuberger, John M.\n\nSieben, Nándor\n\nSwift, James W. https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9433-9456\n\n# Document is current\n\n## Any future updates will be listed below\n\nInvariant polydiagonal subspaces of matrices and constraint programming\n\nCrossref DOI link: https://doi.org/10.1007/s10601-024-09378-3\n\nPublished Online: 2025-01-09\n\nPublished Print: 2024-12\n\n\n  * Authors\n\nNeuberger, John M.\n\nSieben, Nándor\n\nSwift, James W. https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9433-9456\n\n  * License Information\n\nText and Data Mining valid from 2024-12-01\n\nVersion of Record valid from 2024-12-01\n\n\nArticle History\n\nAccepted: 11 December 2024\n\nFirst Online: 9 January 2025\n\nStatements and Declarations\n\n:\n\n: The authors have no conflicts of interest or competing interests.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Bifurcation Lemma for Invariant Subspaces (https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.10448)\nciteturn0academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Neuberger, Nándor Sieben, James W. Swift ... The EBL can be interpreted as the application of the BSE Theorem to a fixed point subspace. ... We present a generalization of the EBL, where the BSE Theorem is applied to nested invariant subspaces.\nTitle: A Bifurcation Lemma for Invariant Subspaces\nAuthors: John M. Neuberger, Nándor Sieben, James W. Swift\nDate: Mon Aug 21 03:46:07 2023\n\nThe Bifurcation from a Simple Eigenvalue (BSE) Theorem is the foundation of steady-state bifurcation theory for one-parameter families of functions. When eigenvalues of multiplicity greater than one are caused by symmetry, the Equivariant Branching Lemma (EBL) can often be applied to predict the branching of solutions. The EBL can be interpreted as the application of the BSE Theorem to a fixed point subspace. There are functions which have invariant linear subspaces that are not caused by symmetry. For example, networks of identical coupled cells often have such invariant subspaces. We present a generalization of the EBL, where the BSE Theorem is applied to nested invariant subspaces. We call this the Bifurcation Lemma for Invariant Subspaces (BLIS). We give several examples of bifurcations and determine if BSE, EBL, or BLIS apply. We extend our previous automated bifurcation analysis algorithms to use the BLIS to simplify and improve the detection of branches created at bifurcations.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSIAM J. APPLIED DYNAMICAL SYSTEMS (https://ac.nau.edu/~jws8/cubePDE/CubePaperSIADS.pdf)\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; NEUBERGER, N. SIEBEN, AND J. ... SWIFT ... In [16] we deﬁned an anomalous invariant subspace (AIS) A ⊆V , with V = BM or H, to\n1244\nJ. M. NEUBERGER, N. SIEBEN, AND J. W. SWIFT\naction on RM to be\nFix(Γi, RM) = {a ∈RM | γ · a = a for all γ ∈Γi}.\nThe ﬁxed-point subspaces for any of the function spaces V = BM, Rn, ˜E, or H are deﬁned as\nFix(Γi, V ) = {u ∈V | γ · u = u for all γ ∈Γi}.\nThere is a one-to-one correspondence between a ∈RM and u ∈BM, and we will often write\nFix(Γi) when the equation is valid for any ambient space. These ﬁxed-point subspaces are\nimportant because they are gs-invariant, meaning that gs(Fix(Γi)) ⊆Fix(Γi). For eﬃciency\nin our code, we restrict gs to one of these ﬁxed-point subspaces, as described in subsection 3.2.\nAs mentioned in the previous subsection, the reduced bifurcation equations are ˜g = 0,\nwhere ˜g : ˜E →˜E is the reduced gradient on the critical eigenspace ˜E. A fold point (a∗, s∗) is\nnot a bifurcation point even though h(a∗, s∗) is singular.\nConsider a bifurcation point (a∗, s∗) in coeﬃcient space, or the corresponding (u∗, s∗), and\nlet Γi = Sym(u∗) be the symmetry of the mother solution. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInvariant synchrony subspaces of sets of matrices (https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.05797)\nciteturn0academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.2 years ago; Neuberger, Nandor Sieben, James W. Swift ... A synchrony subspace of R^n is defined by setting certain components of the vectors equal according to an equivalence relation. ... Applications of these invariant synchrony subspaces include equitable and almost equitable partitions of the vertices of a graph used in many areas of graph theory, balanced and exo-balanced partitions of coupled cell networks, and coset partitions of Cayley graphs.\nTitle: Invariant synchrony subspaces of sets of matrices\nAuthors: John M. Neuberger, Nandor Sieben, James W. Swift\nDate: Thu Aug 15 23:47:23 2019\n\nA synchrony subspace of R^n is defined by setting certain components of the vectors equal according to an equivalence relation. Synchrony subspaces invariant under a given set of square matrices form a lattice. Applications of these invariant synchrony subspaces include equitable and almost equitable partitions of the vertices of a graph used in many areas of graph theory, balanced and exo-balanced partitions of coupled cell networks, and coset partitions of Cayley graphs. We study the basic properties of invariant synchrony subspaces and provide many examples of the applications. We also present what we call the split and cir algorithm for finding the lattice of invariant synchrony subspaces. Our theory and algorithm is further generalized for non-square matrices. This leads to the notion of tactical decompositions studied for its application in design theory.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEquitable partitions of rectangular grid graphs | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/343536/equitable-partitions-of-rectangular-grid-graphs)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Swift, “Invariant synchrony subspaces of sets of matrices”, arXiv:1908.05797 (2020). ... Complete proof: Equitable partitions of finite rectangular grids are symmetry orbits (Neuberger-Sieben-Swift Conjecture 5.6) · PDF.\nThe statement above is taken from the most recent of them; the others are arXiv:1807.10954.\n\nSource: John M. Neuberger, Nandor Sieben and James W. Swift, “Invariant synchrony subspaces of sets of matrices”, arXiv:1908.05797 (2020).\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Refreshed 2 hours ago\n\nOpen · progress reported\n\nA reader-submitted preprint claims a complete proof, but no independent assessment confirms it, so the conjecture remains unsettled.\n\nNeuberger, Sieben, and Swift formulated the conjecture in 2019 after computations suggesting that every equitable partition of a rectangular grid comes from graph symmetries. They reported computations through $m=n=20$m=n=20, but presented the assertion as Conjecture 5.6 rather than a theorem.\n\nKnown results\n\n  * Neuberger, Sieben, and Swift (2019): computational evidence through $m=n=20$m=n=20; no proof or counterexample was given.\n\nCommunity submission (unverified), November 7, 2026\n\nA submitted proof argues that Neuberger, Sieben, and Swift’s Conjecture 5.6 is proved for all $m,n\\ge1$m,n≥1, including the subgroup-lattice criterion and the exception $2\\le m=n\\le3$2≤m=n≤3. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Invariant Synchrony Subspaces of Sets of Matrices. (https://dblp.uni-trier.de/rec/journals/siamads/NeubergerSS20.html)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # \"Invariant Synchrony Subspaces of Sets of Matrices.\" ... Neuberger, Nándor Sieben, James W. Swift (2020)\n\n# \"Invariant Synchrony Subspaces of Sets of Matrices.\"\n\n  * help us\n\n    * How can I correct errors in dblp?\n    * contact dblp\n\nJohn M. Neuberger, Nándor Sieben, James W. Swift (2020)\n\n\n## Details and statistics\n\n  * show external API response\n\n    * JSON @ openalex.org\n\nsee also:\n\n    * API doc @ openalex.org\n\nDOI: 10.1137/19M1283495\n\naccess: closed\n\ntype: Journal Article\n\nmetadata version: 2020-08-25\n\n  * Image: Journal Articles\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI\n      * unpaywalled version\n\n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/siamads/NeubergerSS20\n\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/siamads/NeubergerSS20\n\nJohn M. Neuberger, Nándor Sieben, James W. SwiftImage: 0000-0002-9433-9456:\nInvariant Synchrony Subspaces of Sets of Matrices. SIAM J. Appl. Dyn. Syst. 19(2): 964-993 (2020)\n\n\nPrivacy notice: By enabling the option above, your browser will contact the API of openalex.org to load additional information. Although we do not have any reason to believe that your call will be tracked, we do not have any control over how the remote server uses your data. So please proceed with care and consider checking the information given by OpenAlex.\n\nlast updated on 2020-08-25 16:50 CEST by the dblp team\n\n all metadata released as open data under CC0 1.0 license--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInvariant Polydiagonal Subspaces of Matrices and Constraint Programming (https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.10904)\nciteturn0academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Neuberger, Nándor Sieben, James W. Swift ... In a polydiagonal subspace of the Euclidean space, certain components of the vectors are equal (synchrony) or opposite (anti-synchrony). Polydiagonal subspaces invariant under a matrix have many applications in graph theory and dynamical systems, especially coupled cell networks.\nTitle: Invariant Polydiagonal Subspaces of Matrices and Constraint Programming\nAuthors: John M. Neuberger, Nándor Sieben, James W. Swift\nDate: Sat Nov 16 22:38:40 2024\n\nIn a polydiagonal subspace of the Euclidean space, certain components of the vectors are equal (synchrony) or opposite (anti-synchrony). Polydiagonal subspaces invariant under a matrix have many applications in graph theory and dynamical systems, especially coupled cell networks. We describe invariant polydiagonal subspaces in terms of coloring vectors. This approach gives an easy formulation of a constraint satisfaction problem for finding invariant polydiagonal subspaces. Solving the resulting problem with existing state-of-the-art constraint solvers greatly outperforms the currently known algorithms.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDepartment of Mathematics and Statistics (https://jan.ucc.nau.edu/~jmn3/colloquium_files/swift_012015.pdf)\nciteturn0search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Invariant Subspaces for a Nonlinear PDE on the Sierpinski Gasket. \n \n \n \nAbstract: The Gradient Newton Galerkin Algorithm (GNGA) of Neuberger, Sieben and Swift\nDepartment of Mathematics and Statistics \nCOLLOQUIUM \nTuesday, January 20th, 2015 \n4:00  – 5:00 pm, Adel Mathematics Bldg., Room 164 \n(refreshments at 3:45) \n \nDr. James W. Swift \nNAU \n \n \nInvariant Subspaces for a Nonlinear PDE on the Sierpinski Gasket. \n \n \n \nAbstract: The Gradient Newton Galerkin Algorithm (GNGA) of Neuberger, Sieben and Swift \nuses the invariant subspaces of a nonlinear operator to follow the branching of solutions to a one-\nparameter family of PDEs.  Symmetry of a PDE leads to invariant subspaces, and these are \ncomputed automatically by the suite of GNGA programs.  The PDE on the Sierpinski Gasket has \nan infinite number of invariant subspaces beyond those forced by the symmetry.  This talk will \ndescribe how our incomplete understanding of the anomalous invariant subspaces for the \nSierpinski Gasket can be used to improve the performance of the GNGA. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nAlgebra Combinatorics Geometry and Topology (ACGT) Seminar meets Tuesdays, 12:45 – 1:45 pm, AMB 164. On Tuesday January 20th, Mike \nFalk will talk (after brief organizational meeting) on “The Orlik-Solomon algebra: differential forms and cohomology.” \n \nApplied Math Seminar (AMS) meets Thursdays, 12:45 – 1:45 pm, AMB 164.  On January 22nd, John Neuberger will speak about “Spectral \nDecimation and the Sierpinski Gasket.” \n \nFriday Afternoon Undergraduate Mathematics Seminar (FAMUS) me